  "text": "Question nine. What is a bridge table, and when do you need one? When one fact row relates to several members of a dimension, like an order with two promotions. The fact only has room for one promotion key. So a bridge table holds one row per order and promotion, twenty-four pairs for twelve orders, each with a weight. Join through it and sum the amount, and every order counts once per promotion. Twelve orders worth forty-nine hundred dollars report eleven thousand five hundred five, and nothing looks wrong. Multiply by the weight first. Each order's weights add up to one, so the promotions share exactly forty-nine hundred. The unweighted sums still answer one question, how much revenue each promotion touched. Just never total them.",
